{"name":"Glacial Stream","hex":"#bcd8e2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glacial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, or relating to glaciers.","Very slow."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glacial period (colloquially known as an ice age)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glacial"},{"term":"stream","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small river; a large creek; a body of moving water confined by banks.","All moving waters."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To flow in a continuous or steady manner, like a liquid.","To extend; to stretch out with a wavy motion; to float in the wind."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stream"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glacial stream","blocks":[{"text":"A glacier stream is a channelized area that is formed by a glacier in which liquid water accumulates and flows. Glacial streams are also commonly referred to as \"glacier stream\" or/and \"glacial meltwater stream\". The movement of the water is influenced and directed by gravity and the melting of ice. The melting of ice forms different types of glacial streams such as supraglacial, englacial, subglacial and proglacial streams. Water enters supraglacial streams that sit at the top of the glacier via filtering through snow in the accumulation zone and forming slush pools at the firn zone. The water accumulates on top of the glacier in supraglacial lakes and into supraglacial stream channels. The meltwater then flows through various different streams either entering inside the glacier into englacial channels or under the glacier into subglacial channels. Finally, the water leaves the glacier through proglacial streams or lakes. Proglacial streams do not only act as the terminus point but can also receive meltwater. Glacial streams can play a significant role in energy exchange and in the transport of meltwater and sediment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Different_types_of_Glacier_Streams.jpg/330px-Different_types_of_Glacier_Streams.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glacial_stream","wikidata":"Q2929728"},{"title":"Glacier","blocks":[{"text":"A glacier is a persistent body of natural ice, a form of\nrock, that is constantly moving under its own weight. A glacier forms where the accumulation of snow exceeds its ablation over many years, often centuries. It slowly flows and deforms under stresses induced by gravity, undergoing both ductile and brittle deformation, and acquiring distinguishing surface features, such as crevasses and seracs. As a glacier moves, it abrades underlying rock and debris to create glacial landforms, such as cirques, moraines, and fjords. Although a glacier may flow into a body of water, and can include ice shelves and ice tongues, it originates only on land and is distinct from the much thinner sea ice and lake ice that form on the surface of freezing bodies of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG/330px-Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glacier","wikidata":"Q35666"},{"title":"Stream","blocks":[{"text":"A stream is a continuous body of surface water flowing within the bed and banks of a channel. The channel is a watercourse, although the watercourse may be seasonally or periodically dry. Depending on its location or certain characteristics, a stream may be referred to by a variety of local or regional names. A long, large stream is usually called a river, while a smaller, less voluminous, and more intermittent stream is known as a creek or tributary, among other names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg/330px-%D0%A6%D0%B2%D1%80%D1%86%D0%BA%D0%B0_2_PPRP001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stream","wikidata":"Q47521"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Rupal_River_-_Pakistan.jpg/500px-Rupal_River_-_Pakistan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Rupal_River_-_Pakistan.jpg/500px-Rupal_River_-_Pakistan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rupal River is an example of glacial stream.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARupal_River_-_Pakistan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/500px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/1280px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/500px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Taschachferner in the Ötztal Alps in Austria. The mountain to the left is the Wildspitze (3,768 m), the second highest in Austria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Baltoro_glacier_fr